WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.267
-Welcome back.
-Thank you.

00:00:02.267 --> 00:00:03.334
-Welcome back.
It's been a long time.

00:00:03.334 --> 00:00:04.667
-People.
-I haven't -- yeah,

00:00:04.667 --> 00:00:07.167
there's humans, there's people.
Yeah, that's right!

00:00:07.167 --> 00:00:09.200
-I love it.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:10.601
-You love it, right?
-I love it.

00:00:10.601 --> 00:00:11.767
-It's an amazing --
-Yeah, it's lovely.

00:00:11.767 --> 00:00:12.934
It's lovely.
Yeah, of course.

00:00:12.934 --> 00:00:14.501
-It's an amazing feeling.
Well, I want to say

00:00:14.501 --> 00:00:17.033
you're making
your Broadway debut.

00:00:17.033 --> 00:00:18.300
-I am.
-And I'm very excited.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.701
I cannot wait.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:20.701 --> 00:00:24.167
And not a little challenging,
but you're doing Shakespeare.

00:00:24.167 --> 00:00:26.133
You're doing "Macbeth"?
-Yeah.

00:00:26.133 --> 00:00:27.467
-Wow!
-We can say it here, yeah?

00:00:27.467 --> 00:00:29.167
[ Cheers and applause ]
-What does that mean?

00:00:29.167 --> 00:00:32.334
Yeah, you can say it.
-Well, people are loathe

00:00:32.334 --> 00:00:34.567
to say it in the theater,
because it's --

00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:36.601
-Oh, it's superstitious.
-Superstitious, yeah.

00:00:36.601 --> 00:00:39.167
You have to -- If you say it in
a theater, you have to, like,

00:00:39.167 --> 00:00:41.133
turn around four times,
spit over your shoulder,

00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:43.100
and go out the door
and come back in.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:44.734
-Yeah -- Yeah, exactly right.
Yeah, no.

00:00:44.734 --> 00:00:46.267
-Take your pants off
or something.

00:00:46.267 --> 00:00:48.200
-In here, in the TV show,
you can say --

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:49.501
-You can say it.
"Macbeth," "Macbeth," "Macbeth."

00:00:49.501 --> 00:00:50.501
-"Macbeth," "Macbeth,"
"Macbeth."

00:00:50.501 --> 00:00:52.300
"Macbeth."
But you and Daniel Craig?

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:54.834
-Yeah, I know.
-James Bond -- that's my guy.

00:00:54.834 --> 00:00:56.200
[ Cheers and applause ]
-I know, I know.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:57.300
You're a big fan.
I know.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.167
-I'm an uber fan.
Like, we --

00:01:00.167 --> 00:01:02.033
I was his last interview
as James Bond.

00:01:02.033 --> 00:01:03.734
-Isn't it great?
-We had a martini together.

00:01:03.734 --> 00:01:04.834
-Aww.
-Yeah, it was --

00:01:04.834 --> 00:01:06.133
-Isn't he lovely?
-It was actually --

00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:07.801
it was emotional.
-He's one of the soundest people

00:01:07.801 --> 00:01:09.634
I've ever met.
-He's the great -- Oh, he's --

00:01:09.634 --> 00:01:11.033
Did you get
to rehearse yet or no?

00:01:11.033 --> 00:01:12.467
-We've -- We just did
a couple weeks, yeah,

00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:14.000
sitting around,
talking about it,

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:15.634
and he's just so
down-to-Earth and lovely.

00:01:15.634 --> 00:01:18.734
We bonded over a pencil --
-Pun intended.

00:01:18.734 --> 00:01:19.901
-Oh!
-Thank you.

00:01:19.901 --> 00:01:22.200
-Yes, completely intended.
-That's my style.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.734
-Steal my joke.
"We bonded.

00:01:24.734 --> 00:01:25.968
We bonded over --"
-I have to steal yours.

00:01:25.968 --> 00:01:27.801
I've none of my own.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:27.801 --> 00:01:29.901
-Well, if it gets that reaction,
you won't want to steal it.

00:01:29.901 --> 00:01:31.767
Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:31.767 --> 00:01:33.567
Yeah, fair enough.
Yeah, I'm sorry.

00:01:33.567 --> 00:01:36.567
But what did he -- did you --
Now you can't say the word.

00:01:36.567 --> 00:01:37.934
-Pencil sharpeners.
Parers.

00:01:37.934 --> 00:01:40.300
You know, pencil sharpeners.
Like, stationery.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:41.367
-Really?
-Yeah, he loves --

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:44.067
he loves a good pencil parer.

00:01:44.067 --> 00:01:46.634
[ Laughter ]
So do I -- yeah.

00:01:46.634 --> 00:01:48.567
-Pencil --
-Sharpener, yeah.

00:01:48.567 --> 00:01:51.200
-Yeah, like the --
-It's very satisfying, isn't it?

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:53.834
-Yeah. Like, the -- now they're
battery-powered and stuff.

00:01:53.834 --> 00:01:55.167
And electric.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:01:55.167 --> 00:01:57.767
It's very exciting.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:57.767 --> 00:01:59.434
-No, no, no, no, no.
The old crank.

00:01:59.434 --> 00:02:01.901
-I just ruined
Daniel Craig's reputation.

00:02:01.901 --> 00:02:03.601
[ Laughter ]
The coolest man in the world.

00:02:03.601 --> 00:02:05.200
-The coolest man.
Imagine if a scene

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.000
in "James Bond" is him
sharpening a pencil.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:08.534
[ Laughter ]
No.

00:02:08.534 --> 00:02:10.834
But even he would do it cool.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:02:10.834 --> 00:02:12.801
-He'd, like, come in,
shirt off, and, like --

00:02:12.801 --> 00:02:14.567
-Yeah, yeah.
-Bzzz!

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:16.367
-He's got cool
coming out of his pores.

00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:18.868
It's sort of effortless,
isn't it?

00:02:18.868 --> 00:02:21.834
-Speaking of effortless --
it's a good segue for me.

00:02:21.834 --> 00:02:24.267
You're fascinating and fantastic
in "Passing."

00:02:24.267 --> 00:02:25.901
-Oh, thank you.
-Your film.

00:02:25.901 --> 00:02:28.033
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Thanks a million.

00:02:28.033 --> 00:02:31.267
Means a lot.
-You're getting rave reviews,

00:02:31.267 --> 00:02:32.934
and awards buzz
and all that stuff.

00:02:32.934 --> 00:02:35.133
Do you listen to that or --
-No. Can't.

00:02:35.133 --> 00:02:37.100
-No.
-No, get too nervous.

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:38.434
-Yeah, right?
-Yeah.

00:02:38.434 --> 00:02:40.267
And, you know, it's --
I mean, I'm sure, you know,

00:02:40.267 --> 00:02:43.667
you've heard this before.
That, like, the --

00:02:43.667 --> 00:02:45.601
the good stuff, sort of,
it doesn't stick,

00:02:45.601 --> 00:02:47.200
and the bad stuff,
you're just waking up

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:48.901
in the middle of the night,
tearing your hair out about it.

00:02:48.901 --> 00:02:50.267
-It does.
It is true.

00:02:50.267 --> 00:02:52.200
-I think it's better not to...
-Well, I can tell you,

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.300
as a friend, and after watching
you, you're fantastic in this.

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:56.834
-Thank you so much.
-Yeah, you're really great.

00:02:56.834 --> 00:02:57.934
-Well, I am --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:57.934 --> 00:03:00.234
Thank you.

00:03:00.234 --> 00:03:03.501
-I'm not a critic.
-No, but I -- I am very --

00:03:03.501 --> 00:03:05.868
I'm so proud
to be involved in this film.

00:03:05.868 --> 00:03:08.534
You know, it's Rebecca Hall's
debut and...

00:03:08.534 --> 00:03:10.234
-Yeah.
-...getting to work with her

00:03:10.234 --> 00:03:11.767
and the lovely Tessa Thompson,

00:03:11.767 --> 00:03:13.067
who is extraordinary
in this film, yes.

00:03:13.067 --> 00:03:14.267
-Tessa Thompson
is fantastic, as well.

00:03:14.267 --> 00:03:16.067
-Can we set it up?
Can we explain to everyone

00:03:16.067 --> 00:03:17.267
what the film is about?

00:03:17.267 --> 00:03:22.501
-So, "Passing"
is Rebecca Hall's debut film.

00:03:22.501 --> 00:03:26.634
And she adapted it from a
novella written by Nella Larsen,

00:03:26.634 --> 00:03:29.467
which was published in 1929.

00:03:29.467 --> 00:03:34.701
And it's a story
based on two childhood friends

00:03:34.701 --> 00:03:37.968
who bump into each other
in the lobby of a hotel.

00:03:37.968 --> 00:03:42.300
And one is passing for White,
my character Clare,

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:45.934
and the other
is living in the Black community

00:03:45.934 --> 00:03:48.467
with her Black family.

00:03:48.467 --> 00:03:53.133
And it's about the --
how they're sort of enchanted

00:03:53.133 --> 00:03:56.033
by one another's lives
and the different choices

00:03:56.033 --> 00:04:00.434
they make, and how that
impacts them in the future.

00:04:00.434 --> 00:04:02.000
It's kind of --
-It's -- It's beautiful.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.367
-It is beautiful, isn't it?
-It's absolutely stunning.

00:04:04.367 --> 00:04:05.701
-Yeah.
-And the story is beautiful.

00:04:05.701 --> 00:04:07.734
And every -- and just
the performances, everything,

00:04:07.734 --> 00:04:09.367
just top to bottom.
Congratulations.

00:04:09.367 --> 00:04:11.534
It's so good.
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:04:11.534 --> 00:04:14.100
Here's Ruth Negga
and Tessa Thompson in "Passing."

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:15.534
Take a look at this.

00:04:15.534 --> 00:04:17.334
-Pardon me.
I don't mean to stare,

00:04:17.334 --> 00:04:19.701
